## Closures & Lexical Scope

**Lexical scope** — a function can see variables from where it was **written**, not where it's **called**. Physical location in source code determines access.

```js
function outer() {
  const x = 10;
  function inner() {
    console.log(x); // can see x — written inside outer
  }
}
// If inner() were written outside outer(), it couldn't see x
```
Global variables follow the same rule — everything written inside global scope sees them. Not a special case.

---

**Closure** — an inner function that uses a variable from an outer scope and **outlives** that outer scope.

```js
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;        // outer scope
  return function() {   // inner function outlives makeCounter
    count++;
    return count;
  };
}
const counter = makeCounter(); // makeCounter's stack frame gone
counter(); // 1 — count still alive because closure holds reference
counter(); // 2
```
`count` should be garbage collected — but isn't, because inner function holds a reference. That reference = closure.

**Not just return — any way the inner function outlives outer scope:**
```js
function start() {
  let msg = 'hello';
  setTimeout(() => console.log(msg), 1000); // closure — no return needed
}
```

---

**Closure vs Lexical Scope:**
| | Lexical Scope | Closure |
|---|---|---|
| What it is | Rule about variable visibility | Function keeping outer variables alive |
| Question it answers | Can this function see that variable? | Why is that variable still in memory? |
| When it applies | Always, every function | Only when inner fn outlives outer scope |

> They're not opposites — closure **uses** lexical scope.
> "Can X see Y?" → lexical scope. "Why is Y still alive?" → closure.

---

**The classic `var` bug in loops:**
```js
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  btn.addEventListener('click', function() {
    console.log('Button', i);
  });
}
// Every button logs: Button 5
```
**Why:** `var` is function-scoped — ONE `i` in memory. All 5 handlers close over the **same `i`**. `addEventListener` registers the function for later — does NOT run it immediately. Loop finishes → `i = 5` → all handlers read 5.

> Closures capture the **variable**, not the **value**. If the variable changes, all closures see the change.

**Timing trap:**
| Code | When runs? |
|------|------------|
| `console.log('x')` | Immediately, during loop |
| `addEventListener('click', fn)` | Only on click |
| `setTimeout(fn, 1000)` | After 1 second |

**Fix 1 — `let`** (block-scoped, fresh `i` per iteration):
```js
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('Button', i));
}
```

**Fix 2 — IIFE** (captures `i` by value in a new scope):
```js
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  (function(j) {
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('Button', j));
  })(i);
}
```

> `var` = one variable shared. `let` = fresh variable per iteration. Closures capture the variable, not the value.

