## Prototypal Inheritance & Prototype Chain

**What it is:** JS objects inherit from other objects via a hidden `[[Prototype]]` link. When you access a property, JS walks up the chain until it finds it or hits `null`.

**Chain lookup:**
```js
const animal = { speak() { console.log('...'); } };
const dog = Object.create(animal); // dog.[[Prototype]] = animal
dog.name = 'Rex';
dog.speak(); // not on dog → check animal → found
```

**Constructor functions:**
```js
function Person(name) {
  this.name = name;  // own property — unique per instance
}
Person.prototype.greet = function() {
  console.log('Hi, I am', this.name); // shared — one copy for all instances
};

const p1 = new Person('Utpal');
const p2 = new Person('Alice');
p1.greet === p2.greet; // true — same function via prototype
```

**`new` does 4 things:**
1. Creates a new empty object `{}`
2. Sets its `[[Prototype]]` to `Person.prototype`
3. Runs constructor with `this` = new object
4. Returns the new object

**JS vs Java — property declaration:**
| | Java | JavaScript |
|---|---|---|
| Object shape | Fixed — declared in class | Dynamic — add properties anytime |
| Field declaration | Required | Not needed |
| `this.name = x` | Assigns to pre-declared field | Creates the property on the object |

In JS, `this.name = name` inside a constructor creates the property on the spot — no pre-declaration needed. Objects are dynamic dictionaries.

**`Person.prototype.greet` vs `Person.greet`:**
```js
Person.greet = function() {}         // static — on the function object itself
Person.prototype.greet = function(){}// instance — inherited by all instances

Person.greet();   // static call
p1.greet();       // instance call — found via prototype chain
```
Java equivalent: `Person.greet` = static method, `Person.prototype.greet` = instance method.

**The memory problem — methods in constructor:**
```js
// BAD — 10,000 users = 10,000 copies of getFullName
function User(first, last) {
  this.getFullName = function() { return first + ' ' + last; };
}

// GOOD — one copy shared via prototype
function User(first, last) {
  this.first = first;
  this.last = last;
}
User.prototype.getFullName = function() {
  return this.first + ' ' + this.last;
};
```

> Own properties (data unique per instance) → in constructor via `this`
> Methods (same logic for all) → on prototype

**`class` is just sugar — identical prototype chain underneath:**
```js
class Person {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  greet() { console.log('Hi', this.name); } // goes on Person.prototype automatically
}
```

**`this` in prototype methods:** Determined by who calls it. `p33.getFullName()` → `this = p33` → uses `p33.first`, `p33.last`.

