## this Binding — call, apply, bind

**Core rule:** `this` is determined at **call time**, not definition time. It's the object that calls the function.

**4 rules:**

**1. Implicit binding** — object before the dot:
```js
const user = { name: 'Utpal', greet() { console.log(this.name); } };
user.greet(); // this = user → "Utpal"
```

**2. Explicit binding** — call/apply/bind:
```js
function greet() { console.log(this.name); }
greet.call({ name: 'Utpal' });           // immediate, args comma-separated
greet.apply({ name: 'Utpal' }, []);      // immediate, args as array
const bound = greet.bind({ name: 'Bob' }); // returns new fn, call later
bound();

// apply use case: args already in array
Math.max.apply(null, [1, 5, 3]); // = Math.max(1, 5, 3)
```

| | Calls immediately? | Args format |
|---|---|---|
| `call` | Yes | comma-separated |
| `apply` | Yes | array |
| `bind` | No — returns new fn | comma-separated |

**3. `new` binding** — this = new object:
```js
function Person(name) { this.name = name; }
const p = new Person('Utpal'); // this = newly created object
```

**4. Default binding** — no object, no new:
```js
greet(); // this = window (browser) or undefined (strict mode)
```

**Arrow functions — no own `this`, inherits from lexical scope:**
```js
// WRONG — arrow as method
const user = {
  name: 'Utpal',
  greet: () => console.log(this.name) // this = outer scope (not user) → undefined
};

// CORRECT — arrow inside a method (inherits this from the method)
const user = {
  name: 'Utpal',
  greet() {                      // regular fn — this = user
    const inner = () => console.log(this.name); // inherits this from greet
    inner(); // "Utpal"
  }
};
```
> Rule: methods on objects → regular function. Callbacks inside methods → arrow function.

**Classic trap — setInterval/setTimeout loses `this`:**
```js
class Timer {
  constructor() { this.seconds = 0; }
  start() {
    setInterval(function() {
      this.seconds++; // this = window (default binding) → NaN
    }, 1000);
  }
}

// Fix 1 — arrow function (inherits this from start())
start() {
  setInterval(() => {
    this.seconds++; // this = Timer instance ✓
  }, 1000);
}

// Fix 2 — capture this (pre-ES6 pattern)
start() {
  const self = this;
  setInterval(function() {
    self.seconds++; // closed over — always Timer instance
  }, 1000);
}
```
> Regular function inside setTimeout/setInterval loses `this`. Arrow function captures it from the enclosing method.

