## Promises, async/await & Microtask Queue

**Promise combinators:**

| | Behavior | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `Promise.all` | Fails fast if ANY rejects. Returns values directly. | All must succeed to proceed |
| `Promise.allSettled` | Waits for ALL. Returns `{status, value/reason}` per item. | Partial success is useful (e.g. independent dashboard widgets) |
| `Promise.race` | Resolves/rejects with whichever settles first. | Timeout pattern |

```js
// Promise.all — parallel, fail fast
const [user, orders] = await Promise.all([getUser(), getOrders()]);

// Promise.allSettled — parallel, partial results
const results = await Promise.allSettled([getUser(), getOrders()]);
// [{status: 'fulfilled', value: ...}, {status: 'rejected', reason: ...}]

// Promise.race — timeout pattern
const result = await Promise.race([fetchFromDB(), timeoutAfter(3000)]);
```

**Error handling:**
```js
// .catch() on a Promise
getUser().catch(err => console.error(err));

// try/catch with async/await
async function load() {
  try {
    const user = await getUser();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
  }
}

// Silent failure — no catch = unhandled rejection in production
getUser(); // no await, no .catch() → failure swallowed silently
```

**Sequential vs parallel — the most common interview gotcha:**
```js
// SLOW — 900ms total (each waits for previous)
const profile = await getUserProfile();   // 300ms
const settings = await getUserSettings(); // 300ms after
const notif = await getNotifications();   // 300ms after

// FAST — 300ms total (all run at same time)
const [profile, settings, notif] = await Promise.all([
  getUserProfile(),
  getUserSettings(),
  getNotifications()
]);
```
> Use sequential awaits only when call B **depends on the result** of call A. Otherwise always parallelize with Promise.all.

