## Module System — ES Modules vs CommonJS

**CommonJS (CJS)** — Node.js default, older:
```js
module.exports = { greet, add };
const { greet } = require('./utils');
```

**ES Modules (ESM)** — browser standard, modern Node.js:
```js
export function greet() {}
export default class User {}
import { greet } from './utils.js';
import User from './utils.js';       // default import — name is your choice
import * as utils from './utils.js'; // namespace import
```

**Key differences:**
| | CommonJS | ES Modules |
|---|---|---|
| Syntax | require / module.exports | import / export |
| Loading | Synchronous — blocks | Asynchronous — non-blocking |
| When resolved | Runtime | Parse time (static) |
| Tree shaking | No | Yes |
| Top-level `this` | module.exports | undefined |

**Why ESM is better:**
- Static analysis at parse time → bundlers can tree-shake (remove unused exports)
- Browser native — no bundler needed for basic use
- Standard — future of JS modules

**CJS `require` is dynamic — can't tree shake:**
```js
const mod = require(condition ? './a' : './b'); // decided at runtime
```

**Dual package — supporting both CJS and ESM:**
```json
{
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    }
  }
}
```
Ship two build outputs. Node.js picks the right one automatically. Build tools (tsup, rollup) generate both from one source.

**Gotcha:** You can't `require()` an ESM-only package from CJS. This is why `chalk` v5, `node-fetch` v3 broke CJS projects when they went ESM-only.

> Interview one-liner: "ESM is the standard. For libraries, ship dual CJS+ESM via the `exports` field in package.json."
